confirmed.reasoning:
None of the rule-outs apply.

Not a GTK/GLib limitation faithfully exposed. The C API has no notion of a JS constructor: `g_key_file_new()` is just a function, and the GIR marks the record `disguised`/opaque. Nothing in GLib dictates that a binding must publish a `new`-able class; sibling GI bindings map the type's default constructor onto `new`. The throwing constructor is entirely GTKX's own emission choice, written in `/home/eugenio/gtkx-bughunt/packages/codegen/src/store/gi/record-constructor.ts:46`:

    const renderOpaqueConstructor = (className: string, superCall: string[]): string => {
        const message = sourceStringLiteral(`Cannot construct ${className}: opaque boxed type with no known layout`);
        return renderBlock("constructor()", [...superCall, `throw new globalThis.Error(${message});`].join("\n"));
    };

Not warned against in the GTKX docs. `website/guide/error-handling.md:30` happens to show `GLib.KeyFile.new()`, but showing one correct spelling is not a warning, and the guide teaches `new X()` everywhere else (`new Gtk.FileDialog()`, `new Gio.Cancellable()`, `new Gtk.SingleSelection({...})`). GTKX itself is what invites the mistake: the generated `glib.d.ts:7513` declares a public `constructor();` immediately above `static new(): KeyFile`.

Not a duplicate. F001-F005 in /home/eugenio/gtkx-bughunt/.bughunt/findings.jsonl cover `@gtkx/testing` user-event, `@gtkx/react/adw` AdwAlertDialog children, and `@gtkx/vitest` stray workers. Nothing about codegen record emission.

Not a machine artifact or a stale store. Reproduced from the worktree's own store with no display and no playground involvement: `new GLib.KeyFile/MainLoop/Bytes/Checksum/DateTime/VariantType()` all throw "opaque boxed type with no known layout" while `GLib.KeyFile.new()` succeeds. The store agrees with the codegen source in the worktree, so it is not stale. Scale: 70 of 87 classes in the generated `glib.js` carry the throwing constructor, plus 243 in gtk, 203 in gio, 93 in adw, 31 in gdk, 28 in pango.

Not a missing feature. The narrow defect is not "`new` does not forward to the type's `new` function", it is that the declaration publishes a public constructor that can never succeed, so tsc accepts code the emitter knows will always throw. GTKX already encodes non-constructibility in the type system elsewhere (`isAbstract` gates JSX element emission in `packages/codegen/src/store/jsx/jsx.ts:297`), and the emitter has the discriminator in hand at emission time (`isOpaque(record) || !isConstructibleRecord(...)`), so declaring it is a one-line change it simply does not make. Secondary: `renderRecordConstructorPropsInterface` still emits an unreachable empty `export interface KeyFileConstructorProps {}` (glib.d.ts:8202) for these records, since the opaque constructor takes no props.

